{"sections": [
  {"key": "arc", "title": "The arc", "sentences": [
    {"type": "fact", "text": "Net interest income plus noninterest income rose from $21.0 billion in FY2016 to $28.5 billion in FY2025, a 3.5% CAGR [D5][D219]."},
    {"type": "comparison", "text": "The path was not smooth: revenue was flat at roughly $22.7 billion in FY2021, then jumped 15.8% in FY2023 to $28.0 billion [D117][D169][D189]."},
    {"type": "cause", "text": "That FY2023 step came from higher rates on earning assets and the MUB acquisition, partly offset by deposit mix and pricing [P33]."},
    {"type": "comparison", "text": "FY2024 gave back 2.4% as net interest income fell 6.4% on higher rates pressuring deposit mix and pricing [D214][D209][P21]."},
    {"type": "comparison", "text": "FY2025 recovered 4.4% to $28.5 billion, with net interest income up 2.2% and noninterest income up 7.6% [D239][D219][D234][D235]."}
  ]},
  {"key": "drivers", "title": "What drove growth", "sentences": [
    {"type": "comparison", "text": "Consumer and Business Banking was the largest segment at 31.0% of FY2025 revenue but shrank 3.9%, subtracting 1.3pp from growth [D251][D252][D253]."},
    {"type": "cause", "text": "Its net revenue fell on deposit mix changes and second-quarter loan sales, partly offset by higher mortgage banking revenue [P6]."},
    {"type": "comparison", "text": "Payment Services grew 5.4% to 25.8% of revenue and contributed 1.4pp, with net interest income up on loan growth and lower funding costs [D260][D259][D261][P4]."},
    {"type": "comparison", "text": "Noninterest income growth of 7.6% outpaced net interest income's 2.2%, lifting fee income to $11.9 billion [D235][D234][F20]."},
    {"type": "comparison", "text": "Net income grew 20.2% against 4.4% revenue growth, and EPS grew 21.9% as the diluted share count slipped 0.2% [D227][D239][D228][D237]."}
  ]},
  {"key": "margins", "title": "Margins and costs", "sentences": [
    {"type": "comparison", "text": "The efficiency ratio was 59.0% in FY2025, below its 59.3% window median and far under the 67.4% FY2023 high [D220][D277][D276]."},
    {"type": "cause", "text": "Noninterest expense fell 2.0% to $16.8 billion, reflecting prior-year merger and FDIC charges, lower compensation and cost savings from operational efficiencies [D238][X135][P10]."},
    {"type": "comparison", "text": "The FY2023 spike to 67.4% came from a 26.6% expense increase driven by MUB merger costs and a $734 million FDIC special assessment [D170][D188][P30]."},
    {"type": "comparison", "text": "The credit-loss provision was 7.7% of revenue, near the 6.1% peer median, and fell 2.3% on improved credit quality and loan sales [D221][D285][P12]."},
    {"type": "comparison", "text": "Net income was 26.5% of revenue against a 20.2% tax rate, so the below-the-line drag was modest [D223][D215]."}
  ]},
  {"key": "cash", "title": "Cash conversion", "sentences": [
    {"type": "comparison", "text": "Operating cash flow is not a meaningful earnings-quality measure for a bank, so capital return is the better test [D216]."},
    {"type": "comparison", "text": "Buybacks plus dividends of $3.7 billion were 48.3% of FY2025 net income, up from $2.8 billion in FY2022 [D218][F30][D142]."},
    {"type": "comparison", "text": "Dividends of $3.2 billion dominated that mix while buybacks were only $489 million, versus $4.5 billion in FY2019 [X95][X85][X79]."},
    {"type": "comparison", "text": "Stock-based compensation of $235 million was just 2.9% of operating cash flow, so dilution pressure stayed small [X37][D217]."}
  ]},
  {"key": "balance_sheet", "title": "Balance sheet and capital allocation", "sentences": [
    {"type": "comparison", "text": "Shareholders' equity rose 11.3% to $65.2 billion in FY2025, lifting return on average equity to 12.2% [D233][F114][D226]."},
    {"type": "comparison", "text": "That return ranked in the 71st percentile of seven bank peers against an 11.8% median [D282][D283]."},
    {"type": "comparison", "text": "Loans were 73.5% of deposits, up from 71.8%, as deposits grew 0.8% to $522 billion and loans 3.1% to $384 billion [D224][D199][X212][X211][X202][X201]."},
    {"type": "comparison", "text": "The allowance held at 2.0% of loans, and long-term debt is not reported after FY2022, so leverage beyond that year cannot be tracked [D225][R2][X172]."}
  ]},
  {"key": "red_flags", "title": "Red flags", "sentences": [
    {"type": "fact", "text": "Long-term debt stops being reported after FY2022 at $39.8 billion, a data break that blocks any leverage trend past that year [R2][X172]."},
    {"type": "comparison", "text": "Revenue declined 2.4% in FY2024 as net interest income fell 6.4% on deposit mix and pricing, a one-year dip now reversed [R1][D214][D209][P21]."},
    {"type": "comparison", "text": "The efficiency ratio's 67.4% FY2023 peak was driven by one-off MUB and FDIC charges and has since fallen to 59.0%, so it looks benign [D276][D220][P30]."}
  ]},
  {"key": "watch", "title": "What to watch", "sentences": [
    {"type": "comparison", "text": "If the efficiency ratio stays below the 59.3% window median in the FY2026 10-K, the cost trend described here holds [D277]."},
